<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jebtsundamba Khutughtu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jebtsundamba Khutuktu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jebtsundamba Khutughtu is the title of the highest reincarnate lama of Mongolian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. The line began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanabazar">Zanabazar</a> (1635–1723), son of the Tüsheet Khan, and from the third holder the incarnations were found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. The eighth, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Bogd Khan</a>, became ruler of independent Mongolia in 1911; after his death in 1924 the communist government forbade a successor, though later incarnations were recognised in exile.</p></div>
